## Migration to .NET 8

Your code uses three APIs that throw `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+:
- `Thread.Abort()` → `SYSLIB0006`
- `ThreadAbortException` catch
- `Thread.ResetAbort()`

**Patterns identified:**
1. Cancellable work loop (`while (_running)`)
2. ThreadAbortException as cleanup trigger (`RollbackCurrentTransaction`)
3. ResetAbort to keep the thread alive
4. Blocking call that needs interruption (`GetNextItem()`)

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit loop (replaces ResetAbort + continue behavior)
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts == null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait for the worker to finish gracefully
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (_workerTask != null)
        {
            await _workerTask.WaitAsync(effectiveTimeout)
                .ConfigureAwait(false);
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _workerTask?.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

### Key Changes Required in Dependencies

```csharp
// GetNextItem MUST accept CancellationToken to be interruptible.
// If it blocks on a queue, use a cancellation-aware collection:
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    // BlockingCollection<T>.Take natively supports CancellationToken
    return _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException on cancel
}

// If GetNextItem blocks on Thread.Sleep or WaitHandle:
// Replace Thread.Sleep(ms) → token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)
// Replace event.WaitOne()  → WaitHandle.WaitAny(new[] { event, token.WaitHandle })
```

---

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Not passing token to `GetNextItem`** | The blocking call will never wake up — cancellation stalls indefinitely |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Only catch it when `token.IsCancellationRequested` is true (use the `when` filter) |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers and registered callbacks |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | Cooperative cancellation only fires at checkpoints — add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive operations in `ProcessItem` |
| **Replacing `Thread.Abort` with `Thread.Interrupt`** | Also obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9) — don't use it |

---

### If `GetNextItem` Cannot Be Modified

If it calls unmodifiable third-party blocking code, move it to a child process and use `Process.Kill()` as the last resort — this is the only way to forcibly terminate uncooperative code in modern .NET.
